\begin_layout Description

\lang english
Example 1 :
 i) If a,
 b,
 c form a G.P.
 with common ratio r,
 the product of the y-coordinates of the points of intersection of the line ax+by+c=0 and the currve 
\begin_inset Formula $y^{2}+2x=0$
\end_inset

 .
 ii) Will the intersection coordinates be real if we choose the intersecting curve to be 
\begin_inset Formula $y^{2}=2x$
\end_inset

 .
\end_layout

\begin_layout Quote

\lang english
i)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Quote

\lang english
a) -
\begin_inset Formula $r^{2}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Quote

\lang english
b) -2r
\end_layout

\begin_layout Quote

\lang english
c) -
\begin_inset Formula $2r^{2}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Quote

\lang english
d) r/2
\end_layout

\begin_layout Quote

\lang english
ii)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Quote

\lang english
a) Yes
\end_layout

\begin_layout Quote

\lang english
b) No
\end_layout

\begin_layout Quote

\lang english
c) Can't say
\end_layout

\begin_layout Quote

\lang english
d) None of these.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang english
{ Hint :
 Answer i) c) ii) b) 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang english
Solution :
 i) As a,
 b,
 c are in GP,
 put b =ar,
 c= a
\begin_inset Formula $r^{2}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang english
This gives,
 
\begin_inset Formula $ax+ary+ar^{2}=0$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang english
i.e.
 
\begin_inset Formula $x+ry+r^{2}=0$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang english
putting 
\begin_inset Formula $x=-\dfrac{y^{2}}{2}$
\end_inset

,
 we get
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang english
\begin_inset Formula $-y^{2}+2ry+2r^{2}=0$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang english
Sum of roots =
\begin_inset Formula $y_{1}+y_{2}$
\end_inset

= 
\begin_inset Formula $2r$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang english
Product of roots =
\begin_inset Formula $y_{1}y_{2}$
\end_inset

= 
\begin_inset Formula $-2r^{2}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang english
\begin_inset Formula $|y_{1}-y_{2}|=\sqrt{\left(y_{1}+y_{2}\right)^{2}-4y_{1}y_{2}}=\sqrt{4r^{2}+8r^{2}}=Real$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang english
Hence,
 the equation will have real y-coordinates .
 This can be figured out by drawing the graph of the solution.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang english
ii) In this case,
 
\begin_inset Formula $x=\dfrac{y^{2}}{2}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang english
\begin_inset Formula $y^{2}+2ry+2r^{2}=0$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang english
Sum of roots =
\begin_inset Formula $y_{1}+y_{2}$
\end_inset

= 
\begin_inset Formula $-2r$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang english
Product of roots =
\begin_inset Formula $y_{1}y_{2}$
\end_inset

= 
\begin_inset Formula $2r^{2}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang english
\begin_inset Formula $|y_{1}-y_{2}|=\sqrt{\left(y_{1}+y_{2}\right)^{2}-4y_{1}y_{2}}=\sqrt{4r^{2}-8r^{2}}=Imaginary$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang english
Hence,
 the equation will not have real roots.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang english
}
\end_layout

