\begin_layout Standard

\lang english
,{ Hint :
 By inspection c) (0,0) is the requisite answer .
 It can be further generalized that the orthocenter of the triangle formed by lines x=0,y=0 and any other third line is indeed (0,0).
 } 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Description

\lang english
Example 3 :
 A straight line through the origin divides the parallel lines 
\begin_inset Formula $\sqrt[7]{3}x+5y+8=0$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $3\sqrt[7]{3}x+15y=10$
\end_inset

 in the ratio
\end_layout

\begin_layout Quote

\lang english
a) 8/5
\end_layout

\begin_layout Quote

\lang english
b) 5/8
\end_layout

\begin_layout Quote

\lang english
c) 2/9
\end_layout

\begin_layout Quote

\lang english
d) 0.3
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang english
{Hint :
 Let the line be y=mx
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang english
The point of interesection with the first line would be 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(\dfrac{-8}{\sqrt[7]{3}+5m},\dfrac{-8m}{\sqrt[7]{3}+5m}\right)$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang english
Similarly,
 the point of intersection with the second line would be 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(\dfrac{10/3}{\sqrt[7]{3}+5m},\dfrac{10m/3}{\sqrt[7]{3}+5m}\right)$
\end_inset

 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang english
Using section formula,
 the ratio in which (0.0) divides these the line segment joining these two points would be 8X3/10 = 8/5 }
\end_layout

\begin_layout Description

\lang english
Example 4 :
 The sum of distances of a point from two perpendicular lines is 
\begin_inset Formula $2\sqrt{2}$
\end_inset

 .
 The are enclosed by the locus of these points is.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Quote

\lang english
a) 16
\end_layout

\begin_layout Quote

\lang english
b) 12
\end_layout

\begin_layout Quote

\lang english
c) 32
\end_layout

\begin_layout Quote

\lang english
d) 8
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang english
{ Hint :
 Answer 16 a) 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang english
Let's take the two lines to be x=0 and y=0
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang english
\begin_inset Formula $\Longrightarrow$
\end_inset

 The equation of locus is 
\begin_inset Formula $|x|+|y|=2\sqrt{2}$
\end_inset

 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang english
\begin_inset Formula $\Longrightarrow$
\end_inset

 The equation is basically 4 line segments joining the vertices as a square as for the values 
\begin_inset Formula $x>2\sqrt{2}$
\end_inset

 or 
\begin_inset Formula $y>2\sqrt{2}$
\end_inset

,
 the equality can't hold for real values of x and y.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang english
So,
 the area diagonals are of length 
\begin_inset Formula $4\sqrt{2}$
\end_inset

 each.
 That implies,
 the area of square is 16 Units.}
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection

\lang english
Subjective Problems
\end_layout

\begin_layout Description

\lang english
Example :
 The number of integral points ( integral point means both the coordinates should be integer ) that lie exactly in the interior of the triangle with vertices (0,0),(0,m),(n,0) where m,n are both +ve integers with m>n.
 {Subjective}
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang english
{ Hint :
 By inspection,
 moving down from the topmost point,
 at the line y=m-1,
 the farthermost point inside the triangle is the left hand limit of n/m .
 On this line,
 the integral points is Zero or 
\begin_inset Formula $\left[\dfrac{n}{m}\right]$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang english
On the line y=m-2,
 the number of points is 
\begin_inset Formula $\left[\dfrac{2n}{m}\right]$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang english
Proceeding in this way,
 on the last line with integral points,
 the equation is y=1,
 and the number of points is 
\begin_inset Formula $\left[\dfrac{\left(m-2\right)n}{m}\right]$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang english
Hence ,
 the total number of such points is 
\begin_inset Formula $\left[\dfrac{n}{m}\right]$
\end_inset

 +
\begin_inset Formula $\left[\dfrac{2n}{m}\right]$
\end_inset

+......+ 
\begin_inset Formula $\left[\dfrac{\left(m-2\right)n}{m}\right]$
\end_inset

}
\end_layout

